<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#kris">Glossary</a> · Term · weapon</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kris</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as keris, dhuwung</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The kris or keris is an asymmetrical Javanese dagger whose blade is patterned by laminations of iron and nickelous iron, called pamor; many have a wavy blade, though older ones were mostly straight. Its development most probably took place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, and a Chinese account of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a> describes men from the king to commoners carrying such daggers; the oldest known surviving kris, the Kris of Knaud, bears a date corresponding to 1342. Both weapon and spiritual object, it served as a talisman, an heirloom, part of ceremonial dress and an indicator of social status, and was forged by master smiths called empu. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> listed it as intangible heritage in 2005.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kris.jpg" alt="Kris" width="330" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rijksmuseum · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Javaanse_kris,_AK-MAK-318.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word kris comes from an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Old Javanese</a> term meaning dagger. A Chinese source of the 10th century, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>, records that an envoy from She-po, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, arrived at court in 992 with gifts that included a small dagger with a hilt of rhino horn and gold. Although the peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> already knew stabbing weapons of this type, the kris most probably developed in Java and spread through the archipelago, Malaysia, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> by diplomacy and trade. A Sundanese manuscript dated to 1518 CE describes the kris as the weapon of kings. Claims of forms older than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> kris cannot be verified.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A kris has three parts: the blade (bilah or wilah), the hilt (hulu) and the sheath (warangka), each treated as a work of art in carved wood, metal, gold or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>. Blades are between 38 and 127 cm long and are not normally sharpened, as the weapon is for stabbing. The number of curves, or luk, is always odd, and blades from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> era are straight, as were more than half of all kris before modern times. The pamor pattern comes from iron with a little nickel, perhaps iron ore from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a>, forged in layers.</p><p>The maker was a specialist smith called an empu, a title in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> for those with the skill, and with knowledge of literature, history and the occult as well as spiritual power, since a kris is believed to have a presence of its own. Some blades take years to complete. Heirloom kris, or pusaka, are considered to have good or bad luck, and tales such as that of Empu Gandring's unfinished kris, told in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pararaton">Pararaton</a>, give some of them supernatural powers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Although many assume the kris was the main weapon of fighters, warriors carried it as a secondary arm if they lost their main weapon, usually a spear. Commoners wore it daily, especially when travelling, and in peacetime it formed part of ceremonial dress. Men usually wore one, women a smaller one, and heirloom blades were handed down and worn at weddings and other ceremonies. It signalled status, heroism and authority. Javanese, Balinese and Sundanese traditionally wear it at the back, to show that violence is the last resort, whereas elsewhere in the archipelago it is worn at the front or the left hip.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> the kris dance in the Barong performance shows men in trance stabbing themselves unhurt. Kris-making is preserved in the courts of Yogyakarta and Surakarta and in the principalities of Mangkunegaran and Pakualaman, though it nearly came to a standstill before the 1990s and has since revived. In 2005 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> listed the Indonesian kris on its Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kris” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
